          <p>
            Milk changes the maths more than any powder does. A cup of dairy milk adds about 8 g of protein and roughly 12 g of naturally occurring lactose, along with 100 to 150 calories depending on the fat level. That is why a powder-plus-milk mug reaches 30 to 40 g of protein. If you are counting sugar, note that the lactose is listed as sugar on a label but is not added sugar. Ultra-filtered milks such as Fairlife carry more protein and less lactose per cup, if you want to push the number higher.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calories follow the same pattern. A packet made with water lands near 80 calories. A scoop of whey in a cup of 2% milk is closer to 270. Neither is wrong; they serve different goals. Someone on a calorie deficit who wants a sweet evening drink will be better off with a packet, while someone trying to hit 150 g of protein a day will get more from the powder-and-milk version. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> gives you a daily target to plan around.
          </p>

          <p>
            On price, the gap is large and consistent. Single-serve packets cost two to three times as much per gram of protein as a tub of mainstream chocolate whey, because you are paying for portioning, packaging and a niche brand. If you want packets for convenience, buy the larger canister versions where they exist, use Subscribe &amp; Save on Amazon where it is offered, and compare against the per-serving price of the seven-pack before you click.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fortified sweet drinks can also hide extra sodium. ProtiDIET lists 210 mg per pouch, per the label, and Optimum Nutrition's casein lists 260 mg per scoop. That is modest, but if you are watching salt for blood pressure, add it to your day's total rather than assuming a sweet drink has none.
