        <div id="cocoa-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cocoa and Heavy Metals: What to Know
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cocoa is one of the foods most consistently linked to lead and cadmium. Cacao beans pick up cadmium from the soil they grow in, and lead mostly from dust and soil during fermentation and drying. That is not unique to protein products: dark chocolate bars and cocoa powders have been in the news for the same reason. But it means any chocolate protein drink deserves a second look at the source.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Clean Label Project's 2025 report, which covered 160 protein products, found that chocolate powders had about four times the lead of vanilla on average, and that plant-based powders had roughly three times the lead of whey. A chocolate plant protein combines both risks. Consumer Reports' 2025 and 2026 testing found the same broad pattern, with large differences between individual products. We summarise both in our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the products on this page, the published data is limited. Premier Protein's chocolate whey was one of five chocolate powders in Consumer Reports' January 2026 follow-up, and coverage of those results reported about 0.38 micrograms of lead per serving, roughly 77% of CR's very cautious daily level of concern. We are not aware of independent heavy-metal testing for the dedicated hot cocoa mixes, so we cannot say how they compare. That is not evidence of a problem; it is an absence of evidence either way.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical response is to manage dose rather than panic. One mug of chocolate protein a day is a reasonable ceiling if you also eat chocolate, take other chocolate shakes or use plant protein. Choose dairy-based over plant-based for your cocoa, keep your other shakes vanilla or unflavored, and favour products with published test results. Pregnant people and parents of children should be the most conservative, and should ask their doctor or midwife if unsure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Several brands that make chocolate powders also publish certificates of analysis that include heavy metals. If a brand you like does not, you can email and ask; a company with nothing to hide usually replies. It is the same approach we recommend for any chocolate powder, and it applies equally to a mug of protein hot cocoa.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="careful" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Be Careful
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bariatric patients are one of the main audiences for protein hot cocoa packets, and for good reason: a warm, low-sugar, 15 g drink is easier to sip slowly than a cold shake after surgery. But the timing of when you can have warm liquids, milk and sweeteners depends on your surgical program. Check each product with your surgeon or registered dietitian before you buy, and see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-bariatric-surgery/">protein powder for bariatric surgery</a> guide for the wider picture.
          </p>

          <p>
            People with diabetes will find the packets far lower in sugar than a regular cocoa, at 2 to 3 g per serving on the labels we checked. That does not make them free foods. Milk adds lactose, marshmallows add sugar, and individual responses to sweeteners vary. If you use insulin or medication that can cause lows, test how a new drink affects you, and ask your care team about where it fits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone with chronic kidney disease should talk to a nephrologist or renal dietitian before adding protein drinks, because protein targets in kidney disease are often lower than for healthy adults, and some mixes add potassium. ProtiDIET lists 335 mg of potassium per pouch, per the label. People with phenylketonuria should read every label carefully, since ProtiDIET carries a phenylalanine warning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lactose intolerance is a common and less serious issue. Most of these products use milk proteins, and the packets made with water are typically lower in lactose than a cup of milk, but not lactose-free. If milk bothers you, make the powder versions with lactose-free milk, and look at whey isolates, which contain very little lactose. Our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> guide lists options. This page is general information, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

