        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dumping powder straight into hot milk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dry whey hitting near-boiling milk gels on the outside and traps powder inside, leaving lumps no amount of stirring will fix. Make a paste with two or three tablespoons of cold milk first, then add milk that is steaming but not simmering. It takes twenty seconds and solves almost every clumping complaint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Microwaving after the protein is in</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Microwaves heat unevenly, and the hot spots set whey into rubbery strings. Warm the milk on its own first, then stir it into the paste. If the mug cools too much, reheat in short ten-second bursts with a stir between each, or simply drink it warm rather than very hot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every protein cocoa has the same protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ordinary supermarket cocoa has about 2 g of protein per envelope, some diet cocoas around 12 g, packet mixes 15 g, and GHOST 20 g, per their labels. A chocolate whey in milk can reach 38 g. Read the protein line on the panel, not the word protein on the front of the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying packet prices every day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Single-serve pouches cost roughly $3.50 or more per 25 g of protein at the prices we found, against about $1.50 for a tub of chocolate whey. If you drink protein cocoa nightly through winter, a tub and the cold-paste method will save a significant amount over a season, even after the milk.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking chocolate on chocolate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cocoa is a known source of lead and cadmium, and chocolate powders test higher on average than vanilla. A protein cocoa, a chocolate shake and a chocolate bar in one day adds up. Keep chocolate protein to one serving a day, make your other shakes vanilla, and prefer dairy-based over plant-based cocoa.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

