            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you put protein powder in hot chocolate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Mix the powder with two or three tablespoons of cold milk to form a smooth paste, then slowly add milk heated until steaming but not boiling, stirring as you pour. Adding dry powder to hot liquid is what causes clumps. Casein and milk protein blends tolerate heat better than whey isolate, but all of them work with this method.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does heating protein powder destroy the protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Heat denatures whey, meaning the protein unfolds, which changes texture and causes clumping, but your body breaks protein down during digestion anyway. The amino acids remain and the grams still count. Very high heat for a long time can scorch the drink and spoil the taste, so warm the milk gently and avoid boiling it once the protein is added.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in protein hot cocoa?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most dedicated protein hot cocoa packets on Amazon list 15 g of protein per serving made with water, per their labels, and GHOST lists 20 g. Making them with a cup of dairy milk adds about 8 g more. A scoop of chocolate whey or casein in a cup of milk can reach 32 to 38 g, depending on the product.
                </p>
